---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ukgaap/frs100.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope – Who FRS 100 Applies To" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The UK/Irish Financial Reporting Framework – the Full Suite" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The Decision Tree – Which Standard Applies to Which Entity" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Mandatory EU/UK-Adopted IFRS – Listed Group Consolidated Accounts" },
        { id: "s6", label: "The \"True and Fair\" Override" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Statement Terminology – Dual Naming Conventions Explained" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example – Applying the Decision Tree" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Relationship to IAS/IFRS – a Framework-Level Comparison" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A map of the framework, not a measurement standard itself</div><div class="principle">FRS 100 does NOT itself prescribe RECOGNITION or MEASUREMENT requirements – it is the OVERARCHING standard setting out WHICH financial reporting framework a UK or Republic of Ireland entity should APPLY, and the LEGAL/regulatory hierarchy governing that choice. It was issued by the Financial Reporting Council (FRC) as part of the WHOLESALE overhaul of UK GAAP effective from 2015 – replacing the OLD patchwork of Statements of Standard Accounting Practice (SSAPs), old Financial Reporting Standards, and Urgent Issues Task Force (UITF) Abstracts with a SINGLE, IFRS-ALIGNED suite of standards (FRS 100–105).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope – Who FRS 100 Applies To</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">All entities preparing accounts under UK or Irish company law</div><div class="principle">FRS 100 applies to ALL entities preparing financial statements that are INTENDED to give a true and fair view, and that are NOT REQUIRED to prepare their financial statements in accordance with EU-adopted (or UK-adopted, post-Brexit) IFRS – essentially, it applies to the ENTIRE population of UK and Irish reporting entities, DIRECTING each to the correct component of the "new UK GAAP" suite.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The UK/Irish Financial Reporting Framework – the Full Suite</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Five component standards, plus sector SORPs</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2.2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Role</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2.2fr;"><span>EU/UK-adopted IFRS</span><span>MANDATORY for the CONSOLIDATED accounts of entities with securities admitted to trading on a UK/EU regulated market</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2.2fr;"><span>FRS 101</span><span>Reduced Disclosure Framework – IFRS recognition/measurement, with disclosure exemptions, for qualifying INDIVIDUAL (parent/subsidiary) accounts within an IFRS group</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2.2fr;"><span>FRS 102</span><span>The MAIN standard – based on the IFRS for SMEs Standard – used by the VAST majority of UK/Irish companies, LLPs, and other entities</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2.2fr;"><span>FRS 103</span><span>Insurance Contracts – supplements FRS 102 (or FRS 101) for entities issuing insurance contracts</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2.2fr;"><span>FRS 104</span><span>Interim Financial Reporting – for entities preparing interim reports under the FRS 102/103 suite</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2.2fr;"><span>FRS 105</span><span>Micro-entities Regime – a further-simplified standard for the smallest qualifying companies</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Sector-specific SORPs (Statements of Recommended Practice – e.g. Charities SORP, Further and Higher Education SORP) supplement FRS 102 for particular industries, without themselves being a separate accounting "GAAP."</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Decision Tree – Which Standard Applies to Which Entity</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A sequence of eligibility questions, cascading down the framework</div><div class="principle">An entity works through a SEQUENCE of questions: Is it REQUIRED to use IFRS (e.g. a listed group's consolidated accounts)? If not, does it QUALIFY for FRS 101 (a subsidiary/parent within a publicly-accounts-preparing IFRS group, giving the required notice to shareholders)? If not eligible for – or not electing – FRS 101, does it meet the MICRO-ENTITY size thresholds for FRS 105? If not, or if it chooses NOT to apply the micro-entities regime, it applies FRS 102 (with Section 1A reduced disclosures available if it ALSO qualifies as "small" under the Companies Act).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Mandatory EU/UK-Adopted IFRS – Listed Group Consolidated Accounts</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Full IFRS remains mandatory at the top of the hierarchy</div><div class="principle">UK-incorporated companies (and, separately, Irish-incorporated companies) with securities admitted to trading on a UK or EU REGULATED market must prepare their CONSOLIDATED financial statements using UK-adopted (formerly EU-adopted) IFRS – this requirement sits OUTSIDE and ABOVE the "new UK GAAP" suite entirely; FRS 100–105 govern everyone ELSE, plus the INDIVIDUAL (non-consolidated) accounts of group members even within an otherwise IFRS-reporting group (where FRS 101 often applies instead – Section 6, the companion FRS 101 poster).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>The "True and Fair" Override</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A statutory override available across the entire UK GAAP suite</div><div class="principle">UK and Irish company law requires financial statements to give a TRUE AND FAIR VIEW – in the RARE circumstances where COMPLIANCE with a specific standard's requirement would conflict with giving a true and fair view, the relevant Companies Act provisions permit a DEPARTURE from that requirement (with disclosure of the departure, the reasons, and its effect) – a statutory OVERRIDE mechanism embedded throughout UK/Irish company law, distinct from (though conceptually similar in spirit to) IAS 1's own "fair presentation override," which is used only in extremely rare circumstances internationally.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Statement Terminology – Dual Naming Conventions Explained</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Companies Act names and IFRS-style names are BOTH acceptable throughout this framework</div><div class="principle">Across FRS 100–105, financial statement TITLES follow a genuinely DUAL convention. The Companies Act 2006 (and its associated Regulations) mandates specific STATUTORY FORMATS using the TRADITIONAL British terms: <strong>"BALANCE SHEET"</strong> and <strong>"PROFIT AND LOSS ACCOUNT."</strong> FRS 102 itself (and the wider suite) primarily uses IFRS-STYLE section titles for descriptive purposes – <strong>"STATEMENT OF FINANCIAL POSITION" (SOFP)</strong> and <strong>"STATEMENT OF COMPREHENSIVE INCOME"</strong> (or, if presented in two statements, a separate <strong>"INCOME STATEMENT"</strong> plus a statement of other comprehensive income) – but EXPLICITLY PERMITS an entity to use ALTERNATIVE titles, PROVIDED they are not misleading. In PRACTICE, most UK statutory accounts use the traditional Companies Act titles (Balance Sheet; Profit and Loss Account, often abbreviated <strong>P&amp;L</strong>) given the STATUTORY format requirements, while IFRS-style titles (SOFP; Statement of Comprehensive Income) are also commonly seen, particularly in group reporting packs or where an entity wishes to align presentation with an IFRS-reporting parent. BOTH sets of terms describe the SAME underlying statements and are used INTERCHANGEABLY throughout this poster series.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example – Applying the Decision Tree</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Tracing an entity through the framework</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A UK subsidiary of a listed IFRS group</div><div class="wk-body">A private UK trading subsidiary is wholly owned by a UK-listed parent that prepares publicly available consolidated accounts under UK-adopted IFRS. The subsidiary is not itself a financial institution, and has given the required notice to its shareholder (the parent) of its intention to apply reduced disclosures, without objection.</div><div class="wk-answer">Since the subsidiary is NOT itself required to use IFRS (only the LISTED PARENT's consolidated accounts carry that mandatory requirement), and it MEETS the FRS 101 qualifying conditions (member of an IFRS group preparing publicly available consolidated accounts, notice given, no objection), the subsidiary may elect to prepare its INDIVIDUAL accounts under <strong>FRS 101</strong> – using IFRS recognition and measurement, with REDUCED disclosures, rather than the FULL disclosure burden of standalone IFRS OR the different (though converged) FRS 102 measurement basis.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What FRS 100 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Identification of the financial reporting framework applied, and confirmation of compliance</span><span>Where the true and fair override is invoked, disclosure of the departure, reasons, and effect</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether an entity qualifies for FRS 101 (group membership, notice requirements, no shareholder objection)</span><span>Whether the FRS 105 micro-entity size thresholds are met, and whether to elect that regime if eligible</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Generally minimal at the FRS 100 level – this is a framework-selection Topic, not a measurement Topic</span></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS – a Framework-Level Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A genuinely IFRS-anchored hierarchy, at every level</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>UK GAAP layer</span><span>IFRS relationship</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Mandatory IFRS (listed consolidated accounts)</span><span>Full UK-adopted IFRS itself – IDENTICAL to the international standards, subject to the UK's own endorsement process post-Brexit</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>FRS 101</span><span>FULL IFRS recognition/measurement, with disclosure EXEMPTIONS only – not a different measurement basis</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>FRS 102</span><span>Based on the IFRS FOR SMEs Standard, with UK/Irish company law amendments and some RETAINED UK-specific policy options</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>FRS 105</span><span>A FURTHER simplification of FRS 102's already-simplified IFRS-for-SMEs basis</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Every tier of "new UK GAAP" is explicitly anchored to, or derived from, the IFRS family – this is a genuinely DIFFERENT relationship than US GAAP's historically INDEPENDENT (FASB-originated) development, even where the two have subsequently converged on many specific topics.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where FRS 100 itself has an effect</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Framework selection itself</span><span>None directly</span><span>FRS 100 determines WHICH standard applies; it has no measurement effect of its own</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>FRS 101</span><span>The Reduced Disclosure Framework FRS 100 directs qualifying entities toward</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>FRS 102</span><span>The main standard FRS 100 directs most entities toward</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>FRS 105</span><span>The micro-entities regime FRS 100 directs the smallest qualifying entities toward</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">True and fair view</div><div class="glossary-def">The overriding statutory requirement of UK/Irish company law that financial statements present a true and fair view, permitting a departure from specific standard requirements in rare cases where compliance would conflict with it.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Qualifying entity</div><div class="glossary-def">A subsidiary or parent within a group preparing publicly available consolidated IFRS accounts, eligible to apply FRS 101's reduced disclosures.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">SOFP / Balance Sheet</div><div class="glossary-def">The statement of financial position – termed "Statement of Financial Position" (SOFP) in IFRS-style usage, or "Balance Sheet" under the traditional Companies Act statutory format, both acceptable.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.frs100 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
